              "model_answer": "A primary catalyst that detonated the rebellion in May 1857 was the profound religious and cultural anxiety provoked by the introduction of the Pattern 1853 Enfield rifle cartridge. As detailed in paragraphs [3.1] and [3.2], sepoys had to bite open cartridges greased with animal tallow derived from cows (sacred to Hindus) and pigs (abhorrent to Muslims). In an army where soldiers had already witnessed intrusive Christian missionary activity and overseas service laws threatening their ritual purity, the greased cartridge was perceived not as a minor technical reform, but as a deliberate attempt to defile their faith. When soldiers at Meerut were court-martialled and shackled for refusing the cartridges, their comrades rose in armed revolt, marching on Delhi to proclaim Emperor Bahadur Shah Zafar.<br><br>However, attributing the 1857 uprising solely to bullet grease ignores the profound structural grievances that had accumulated over a century of East India Company rule. As demonstrated in Block 1, ever since the 1765 Treaty of Allahabad (Source A) granted the Company the Diwani of Bengal, corporate directors had extracted millions of pounds in land revenue to purchase Indian goods with Indian taxpayers' own money. Furthermore, the early 19th century witnessed the deliberate de-industrialisation of the subcontinent: unequal British import tariffs and the flood of cheap machine-woven Lancashire cotton ruined millions of traditional handloom weavers in ancient centers like Dhaka [2.1, 2.2]. As Governor-General Bentinck confessed in 1834, the misery of starving artisans was without parallel. When the uprising began, ruined craftsmen and over-taxed zamindars joined the sepoys, as articulated in the Azamgarh Proclamation (Source C).<br><br>This economic destruction was compounded by aggressive political dispossession under Governor-General Lord Dalhousie. Through the ruthless Doctrine of Lapse (1848–1856), Dalhousie refused to recognize adopted heirs, unilaterally annexing historic states such as Satara, Nagpur, and Jhansi [2.3]. By dispossessing Rani Lakshmibai of Jhansi and annexing the kingdom of Awadh—the principal recruitment homeland of Bengal sepoys—Dalhousie alienated both Indian royalty and the rural military classes. Rani Lakshmibai’s armed resistance on horseback (Source B) exemplified how aristocratic dispossession fused with military mutiny into a coordinated struggle for regional sovereignty.<br><br>Ultimately, while the Enfield rifle cartridge was the immediate spark that detonated the rebellion at Meerut, it was the explosive fuel of a century of corporate tax plunder, handloom de-industrialisation, and Dalhousie’s predatory annexations that sustained the conflict. As evidenced by Parliament’s decision to dissolve the East India Company and establish the direct British Raj in 1858 (Source D), the British state recognized that corporate greed had pushed India to the brink of total revolution. Therefore, the 1857 uprising was fundamentally a broad war of national resistance against predatory corporate colonialism."
