              "model_answer": "From an Indigenous and post-colonial historical perspective, the British colonization of Australia between 1788 and 1854 was fundamentally an era of systematic, violent dispossession rather than peaceful pioneering settlement. As detailed in Block 2, the foundational injustice of British rule was the legal fiction of terra nullius (Source B). By legally declaring a continent that had been continuously inhabited and managed by over 250 Indigenous nations for 65,000 years to be \"nobody’s land\", the British Crown legitimized the total seizure of ancestral soil without negotiating treaties, purchasing land, or offering compensation. This legal erasure reduced sovereign Indigenous peoples to trespassers on their own country, laying the groundwork for unconstrained colonial violence.<br><br>Furthermore, as documented in Block 3 and Source C, British settlement along the Hawkesbury and Parramatta rivers ignited the Australian Frontier Wars. Far from passively fading away, Indigenous nations mounted fierce, organized resistance. Led by Bidjigal warrior Pemulwuy, Eora fighters waged a twelve-year guerrilla war, burning crops, ambushing supply lines, and resisting British infantry detachments with tactical ingenuity [3.2, 3.3]. The British response was ruthless: Governor King declared Pemulwuy an outlaw, military expeditions carried out punitive raids, and when Pemulwuy was killed in 1802, his head was severed and sent to London as an imperial trophy. When combined with deadly smallpox epidemics, colonial settlement was experienced by Indigenous communities as an armed military invasion that decimated ancient populations.<br><br>The violence of dispossession intensified between 1820 and 1850 with the pastoral wool boom. Pastoral squatters drove millions of fine-wool merino sheep across New South Wales and Victoria, destroying native yam beds, draining sacred waterholes, and displacing Indigenous clans [4.1]. In Van Diemen’s Land (Tasmania), the Black War resulted in the virtual near-extinction of the Palawa people under martial law. While the 1851 Victorian Gold Rush and the 1854 Eureka Stockade (Source D) won celebrated democratic reforms—such as universal male suffrage and the secret ballot—this emerging democracy was exclusively white and settler-dominated. Settler self-government solidified pastoral land grabs, leaving Indigenous Australians dispossessed and excluded from civil rights.<br><br>Ultimately, while 19th-century British narratives romanticized Australia as a heroic triumph of convict redemption and pastoral progress, forensic historical evidence reveals that every town, sheep station, and goldfield was founded upon the violent displacement of Indigenous nations. As Captain Watkin Tench admitted as early as 1793, Indigenous warriors fought with resolute courage to defend their ancestral soil against foreign invasion. Therefore, the colonization of Australia was fundamentally characterized by armed dispossession under the shield of a fraudulent legal fiction."
