              "model_answer": "From an economic and post-colonial historical perspective, the Scramble for Africa between 1884 and 1914 was overwhelmingly driven by an insatiable lust for natural resources and corporate profit. As documented in paragraph [1.3] and Source D, European powers did not partition Africa to assist its inhabitants; they sought raw industrial commodities like rubber, palm oil, copper, gold, and diamonds to sustain their domestic factories. When European diplomats met at the 1884 Berlin Conference (Source A), they established the rule of 'Effective Occupation', which triggered a frantic military race to seize resource-rich interior territories [1.2]. To achieve this extraction, commercial syndicates like Cecil Rhodes's British South Africa Company used extreme coercion and corporate police to dispossess indigenous peoples of their ancestral land and herd them into mines [3.2]. The devastating deployment of the Maxim machine gun at Omdurman, where 11,000 Sudanese warriors were mowed down in a few hours, demonstrated that imperial expansion was fundamentally an act of armed resource theft rather than a diplomatic partnership [2.3].<br><br>Conversely, imperial apologist historians argue that cultural, strategic, and moral motives also played a significant role in motivating the European public and missionary societies. As evidenced in Rudyard Kipling's famous 1899 poem 'The White Man's Burden' (Source C), millions of Victorian Britons genuinely believed they were engaged in a noble 'civilising mission'. Evangelical humanitarians advocated expansion into the interior to suppress the Arab-Swahili slave trade, spread Christian education, and establish modern hospitals and schools [3.1]. Furthermore, British geopolitical strategy was heavily driven by the desire to secure global maritime trade routes, particularly the Suez Canal in Egypt and the naval coaling stations at Cape Town, rather than purely local profits [4.1]. Defenders also point out that British colonial rule established modern legal codes, postal networks, and agricultural research stations that integrated African territories into global commerce [4.2].<br><br>Ultimately, in evaluating the true drivers of the Scramble for Africa, a historian must distinguish between the public propaganda that justified empire and the economic mechanisms that financed it. While sincere missionaries and administrators sought to bring education and medicine, their efforts were entirely subordinate to the commercial priorities of mining syndicates and industrial cartels. As E. D. Morel forensicly proved in Source D, the rhetoric of 'the white man's burden' served as a convenient moral mask to obscure the brutal reality of forced labour, military atrocities, and resource extraction. Therefore, the Scramble for Africa was fundamentally driven by the insatiable resource demands of European industrial capitalism."
